; cow-arena.service — managed by database/cron/install-timers.sh
; (do not edit the installed copy in ~/.config/systemd/user; edit THIS template,
; commit, and redeploy).
;
; __REPO_DIR__ is substituted with the absolute repo path at install time, so the
; unit never depends on the checkout living at any particular path.
;
; PROD ONLY. Dev is intentionally left with no schedule — trigger a pass by hand
; with `npx tsx scripts/arena-tick.ts` from server/. See docs/ARENA_FEATURE.md § 10
; and docs/ARENA_DEPLOY_RUNBOOK.md.

[Unit]
Description=Arena hourly pass (resolve closed arenas, then form new ones)
Documentation=file://__REPO_DIR__/docs/ARENA_FEATURE.md
; Nothing to order against: the job talks to the backend container, not a host
; service, and it tolerates the container being down (it fails, logs, retries next
; hour). It is deliberately NOT ordered after cow-maintenance.service — see below.

[Service]
Type=oneshot

; ── WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE UNIT AND NOT A THIRD ExecStart ON cow-maintenance ───
; cow-maintenance is a oneshot with two ordered steps, and systemd ABORTS the unit
; when a step fails. That is correct there, because its step 2 consumes what its
; step 1 writes. The arena shares nothing with either: it reads opt-ins and writes
; arenas/arena_members, while the maintenance pass debits minute points and prunes
; templates. Appending it there would mean a failed inactivity-penalty SQL run
; silently prevents arenas from forming — and because formation only happens in the
; hours around the Tuesday boundary, a failure in the wrong hour costs a whole week
; of arenas for every user. Separate units keep those failure domains apart.
;
; A oneshot is still a SINGLETON, so overlapping runs remain impossible: if a pass
; somehow ran past the hour, systemd will not start the next one on top of it.
;
; Runs as COMPILED JS inside the backend container — the prod image has no tsx and
; no devDeps, exactly as the template-prune step does. `bash -c` is required for
; the `>>` append, which is shell syntax systemd's ExecStart does not interpret.
;
; ⚠️ The script is arena-cron.js, NOT arena-tick.js. arena-tick is the dev trigger
; and accepts --seed-opt-ins, which would opt every user in the database into next
; week. Never point a scheduler at it.
ExecStart=/bin/bash -c '/usr/bin/docker exec cow-backend-prod node dist/scripts/arena-cron.js >> __REPO_DIR__/logs/arena-cron.log 2>&1'

; The script exits non-zero on any failure, so a bad run shows up in
; `systemctl --user status cow-arena` and in the journal rather than only in the
; log file: `journalctl --user -u cow-arena`.
